Chapter: FieldAssignUnobservable

 A field assignment whose receiver names a top-level definition rather than
 a binding. The definition is a function of no arguments, so `counter` here
 evaluates its body again at every mention and builds a fresh Counter: the
 write in `bump` lands in a record nothing else holds, and `read` gets the
 initial zero. Both the write and the read type-check, and the program runs
 without complaint -- it just answers 0 forever.

 This is the shape that made `a64-effect-cell` dead for its whole life and
 that silently swallowed an SMP flag in CL 9208. CDX2068 rejects it.

Section: The Cell That Is Not One

  mutable Counter = record {
   n : Integer
  }

  counter : Counter = Counter { n = 0 }

  bump : Integer -> Integer
  bump (x) =
   let stored = counter.n = x
   in x

  read-back : Integer
  read-back = counter.n

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let w = bump 7
   in print-line (show read-back)
  end
